{"question": "A 66-year-old man presents to the emergency department with abdominal pain, nausea, and vomiting. He endorses diffuse abdominal tenderness. His past medical history is notable for diabetic nephropathy, hypertension, dyslipidemia, depression, and morbid obesity. He also is currently being treated for an outbreak of genital herpes. His temperature is 99.0\u00b0F (37.2\u00b0C), blood pressure is 184/102 mmHg, pulse is 89/min, respirations are 18/min, and oxygen saturation is 98% on room air. Physical exam is notable for an obese man in no acute distress. A CT scan of the abdomen with contrast is performed and is unremarkable. The patient is admitted to the observation unit for monitoring of his pain. Notably, the patient's abdominal pain improves after an enema and multiple bowel movements. The patient's evening laboratory values are ordered and return as seen below.\n\nSerum:\nNa+: 141 mEq/L\nCl-: 99 mEq/L\nK+: 4.8 mEq/L\nHCO3-: 11 mEq/L\nBUN: 20 mg/dL\nGlucose: 177 mg/dL\nCreatinine: 3.1 mg/dL\n\nWhich of the following is the most likely etiology of this patient's laboratory derangements?", "options": {"A": "Acyclovir", "B": "Atorvastatin", "C": "Insulin", "D": "Metformin", "E": "Metoprolol"}, "pred_answer": "A", "gold_answer": "D", "question_domains": ["Emergency Medicine", "Nephrology", "Gastroenterology", "Endocrinology", "Infectious Disease"], "option_domains": ["Antiviral and Metformin Management", "Statin and Beta-Blocker Management"], "question_analyses": {"Emergency Medicine": "The patient's laboratory derangements, particularly the elevated creatinine and metabolic acidosis, are most likely due to acute kidney injury (AKI). The patient's history of diabetic nephropathy and hypertension, along with the elevated creatinine (3.1 mg/dL, normal range 0.6-1.2 mg/dL), suggest that his kidneys are not functioning optimally. The metabolic acidosis, indicated by a low bicarbonate level (11 mEq/L, normal range 22-28 mEq/L), further supports this diagnosis